{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially beyond ~day 10. Apply a downward level shift (increasing with horizon).",
    "Trend/turning-point miss: when the market transitions into a drawdown, the model keeps projecting flat-to-higher prices (fails to capture the downtrend). Add a regime filter: if recent returns are negative and volatility rising, tilt the entire path downward.",
    "Overshoot after local highs: following a short-term spike/new high in history, the model extrapolates strength, but outcomes mean-revert lower. Use mean-reversion: cap upside continuation and pull forecasts back toward a rolling mean after spikes.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small; mid/long horizon (days 10–30) errors become large and one-sided. Increase correction magnitude with lead time (e.g., linear ramp of downward adjustment).",
    "Volatility miscalibration: forecast paths are too smooth and stay elevated, while realizations show sharper sell-offs. Introduce asymmetric downside risk: widen lower bands / skew distribution downward when recent momentum is negative."
  ]
}
